From ebc9eb327470dbc2d88363ca3631216dcdfa83b3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nicole Muzzin <nicks@fuckin.email>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 06:46:42 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] image cache: paint partially loaded JPEG and PNG images

Images showed nothing until the whole file had downloaded and decoded, so on
slow connections pages stayed blank where Chrome already shows the top of a
baseline JPEG or a blurry pass of a progressive one.

While an image downloads, the image cache now decodes the bytes received so
far at most every 100 ms (loads that finish sooner never pay for it) and
publishes the result under one WebRender key, which the complete image takes
over so paint swaps the pixels in place. zune-jpeg decodes a truncated stream
with the missing blocks as mid-gray, and the PNG decoder stops where the data
ends, so the rows not yet received are cut off and left transparent like in
Chrome. Animated GIF/WebP/APNG, interlaced PNG, 16-bit images, SVG and the
other formats are still shown only once complete.

The partial image reaches <img> through a new ImageResponse::PartiallyDecoded,
which keeps the request partially available: complete stays false, load fires
at the end as before, and canvas, WebGL and createImageBitmap still refuse to
read it. CSS images get it from ImageCache::get_partially_decoded_image and
stay registered for the completion notification.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
